WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.987
Structure: tags or headings, used consistently.

2
00:00:03.267 --> 00:00:07.533
A real prompt mixes instructions, background, examples, and data.

3
00:00:07.813 --> 00:00:13.915
If they run together, the model has to guess which sentences are commands and which are material to work on.

4
00:00:14.195 --> 00:00:15.859
Delimiters remove the guess.

5
00:00:16.139 --> 00:00:25.504
Anthropic recommends XML tags with descriptive names, such as <instructions>, <context>, and <document>, nested where the content has a hierarchy.

6
00:00:25.784 --> 00:00:32.141
OpenAI’s guide recommends a mix of Markdown headings and XML tags to mark boundaries.

7
00:00:32.421 --> 00:00:41.211
Google’s Gemini guide says XML-style tags or Markdown headings both work, and to pick one and use it consistently within a prompt.

8
00:00:41.491 --> 00:00:42.835
Tags pay off twice.

9
00:00:43.115 --> 00:00:54.571
They help the model parse the prompt, and they help you: you can refer to “the contract in the document tags” in your instructions, ask for output inside named tags, and extract it with code.

10
00:00:54.851 --> 00:01:05.411
Anthropic’s tutorial devotes a whole chapter to this separation of data from instructions, because it is the step that turns a one-off prompt into a reusable template.

11
00:01:05.691 --> 00:01:10.213
Templates raise an engineering issue the tutorials mention only briefly.

12
00:01:10.493 --> 00:01:17.853
When you insert text you didn’t write (a customer email, a scraped page, a ticket), that text might contain your closing tag.

13
00:01:18.133 --> 00:01:19.328
Escape it first.

14
00:01:19.608 --> 00:01:26.691
Replacing &, <, and > with their entities means a ticket that says </ticket> can no longer end the ticket block early.

15
00:01:26.971 --> 00:01:29.616
You will write that function in the exercise below.
